includes:
    - vendor/larastan/larastan/extension.neon

parameters:
    # Level 2, and the level is the whole decision, so it is written down.
    #
    # Level 0 found fifteen problems and every one was real: a relation on a
    # live model pointing at a class that does not exist, two env() calls
    # that stop working the moment `config:cache` runs, and three dead
    # Laravel-5-era classes that could not even be loaded. Level 1 cost one
    # more fix.
    #
    # Level 2 was 120 errors, and they were not 120 problems. 113 were one
    # root cause: reading an attribute or calling a method on a value
    # PHPStan infers as the generic Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model,
    # because the relations it came through carried no generic annotation.
    # `getContestTime()` exists; the annotation did not. Annotating the
    # relations took the count from 120 to 15 -- one `@return BelongsTo<
    # Problem, $this>` on Run::problem() alone removed 23 -- and the
    # fifteen that survived were each a real statement about the code:
    #
    #   - Contest::getEndTimeAttribute() declared \DateTime and its own body
    #     called ->copy(), which \DateTime does not have;
    #   - EventImporter took `class-string<Model>` for a helper only ever
    #     called with three soft-deletable models, so withTrashed(),
    #     trashed() and restore() had nothing to resolve against;
    #   - JudgeController set an undeclared `is_overdue` attribute purely so
    #     a Blade file could read it back;
    #   - PracticeController read four query-selected aliases as if they
    #     were columns of practice_publications.
    #
    # No baseline, at any level. A baseline says "these errors are
    # acceptable" and then nobody reads the list again.
    #
    # Level 3 was 128 errors before the relation annotations and NINE after,
    # which is why it was worth measuring instead of estimating. Of those
    # nine, eight were `@var array` docblocks on Laravel-5-era Helium\
    # classes -- and the right fix was not to annotate them. Every one was
    # dead: a Http\Kernel and a Console\Kernel that Laravel 11+ replaced
    # with bootstrap/app.php, three middleware classes nothing referenced,
    # and four service providers that bootstrap/providers.php already
    # documented as unbootable. Deleted; giving a precise type to a file
    # that cannot boot is not an improvement.
    #
    # The ninth was Carbon stub imprecision rather than a defect, and it is
    # worth knowing why the fix looks the way it does:
    # Contest::getEndTimeAttribute() really does return an
    # Illuminate\Support\Carbon -- verified by running it -- but Carbon's
    # own stubs type addMinutes() as returning the parent Carbon\Carbon, so
    # PHPStan could only prove the parent. Widening the declared type would
    # have silenced it by making the type less true than reality, so the
    # method converts explicitly instead (a no-op at run time).
    #
    # Level 4 was measured too, and the recommendation is to STOP HERE.
    # It is 32 errors, and unlike levels 0-3 they are mostly the analyser
    # being pedantic about types it has already narrowed -- 16
    # `nullsafe.neverNull`, a handful of "this comparison is always true".
    # Worse, three of them are wrong: PHPStan types a by-reference closure
    # capture (`use (&$block)`) as its value at closure-creation time, so
    # TeamFileParser::parseBocaUsers() looks like `$block === []` is always
    # true, the code after it unreachable, and appendBocaBlock() unused.
    # None of that is so -- tests/Unit/Services/TeamFileParserTest.php
    # asserts on a message that only appendBocaBlock() produces. Raising to
    # level 4 would mean restructuring correct code to satisfy an analysis
    # that is mistaken about it, which is a worse trade than levels 2 and 3,
    # where every finding was real.
    #
    # The one level-4 finding worth having was taken anyway, without raising
    # the level: PracticeController injected a PracticePublisher it never
    # read.
    level: 3

    paths:
        - app
